"""Literature evidence for the centralization codebook: Old Akkadian / Sargonic (c. 2316-2200 BC).
Format as 81_lit_evidence_ur3.py. Quotations copied from OHANE I ch. 9 (Schrakamp), which has no printed page numbers in the
PDF; cited by section and PDF page. Read by 85_build_scores.py."""
P='Old Akkadian'
S='schrakampKingdomAkkadView2020'  # Schrakamp, I. 2020. The kingdom of Akkad: a view from within. OHANE I, ch. 9.
E=[
 dict(param='TERR',score=3,stance='supports',key=S,where='OHANE I, ch. 9, §9.1 (PDF p. 541)',
  quote="Through the defeat of Lugalzagesi, Sargon unified the whole of southern Mesopotamia for the first time into a single territorial state and laid the foundations of the kingdom of Akkad, which dominated large parts of the Near East for more"),
 dict(param='TERR',score=3,stance='supports',key=S,where='OHANE I, ch. 9, §9.6 (PDF p. 580)',
  quote="the consolidation of the kingdom of Akkad constituted the decisive first step in a process that resulted in the long-term implementation of the concept of the territorial state in the Mesopotamian political landscape."),
 dict(param='HIER',score=3,stance='supports',key=S,where='OHANE I, ch. 9, §9.3 (PDF p. 547)',
  quote="Sargon claims to have razed the fortifications of the conquered cities and to have appointed “Sons of Akkad” (dumu-dumu A-ga-de3ki) as governors as far as the Gulf, thus turning former city states into provinces directly answerable to the king."),
 dict(param='HIER',score=3,stance='supports',key=S,where='OHANE I, ch. 9, §9.4 (PDF p. 567)',
  quote="On a provincial level, they acted as the highest royal administrators, while within the state hierarchy they were answerable to higher-ranking royal officials outside of the provincial system."),
 dict(param='HIER',score=2,stance='contests',key=S,where='OHANE I, ch. 9, §9.4 (PDF p. 557), citing Westenholz 1984b, 1999 vs Foster 1982d, 2016',
  quote="While some scholars emphasize that the Sumerian governors under Naram-Sin and Šar-kali-šarri had local origins and assume that they were granted a considerable degree of independence, others highlight that they were appointed by the king and postulate that they merely administered on his behalf the provinces, which they assume were tightly integrated into the tributary state economy.",
  note='An explicit scholarly debate (Westenholz vs Foster).'),
 dict(param='HIER',score=2,stance='qualifies',key=S,where='OHANE I, ch. 9, §9.3 (PDF p. 550)',
  quote="local administrative practices continued, indicating that the lower ranks of the regional administration remained in place despite the regime change."),
 dict(param='FISC',score=2,stance='supports',key=S,where='OHANE I, ch. 9, §9.3 (PDF p. 555)',
  quote="The allocation of land generated and deepened loyalties and dependencies toward the crown among the beneficiaries, who were expected to pay taxes and muster conscripts as appropriate to their landholdings."),
 dict(param='FISC',score=3,stance='qualifies',key=S,where='OHANE I, ch. 9, §9.4 (PDF p. 567)',
  quote="A characteristic feature of their archives is the coexistence of local administrative practice with obvious interference from the royal administration, which is particularly evident in the administration of agricultural land, obligations to deliver goods to the crown, and the mobilization of corvée troops ... The later poem “Curse over Akkad” preserves a surprisingly fitting literary reminiscence, stating that under Sargonic rule, the governors, temple administrators, and cadastral officials had to send taxes to Akkad.",
  note='The explicit statement of central taxation is a later Ur III literary text; there is no Sargonic equivalent of the bala.'),
 dict(param='COMM',score=2,stance='supports',key=S,where='OHANE I, ch. 9, §9.4 (PDF p. 561)',
  quote="they appear as members of the royal entourage in texts related to the “Royal Progress,” the journeys that king and court undertook throughout the realm in order to scrutinize and ascertain the loyalty of the local governors."),
 dict(param='COMM',score=2,stance='supports',key=S,where='OHANE I, ch. 9, §9.4 (PDF p. 560)',
  quote="the documents of the governor’s archive mention numerous royal functionaries as well as messengers and dignitaries from other provinces within the kingdom of Akkad"),
 dict(param='PAL',score=3,stance='supports',key=S,where='OHANE I, ch. 9, §9.3 (PDF p. 551)',
  quote="Taken together, the fragments document at least 1,339 sq km of land ... This constitutes the largest agricultural domain ever attested in the cuneiform record, in all likelihood corresponding to approximately 2 percent of land under the plow in Lower Mesopotamia."),
 dict(param='PAL',score=3,stance='supports',key=S,where='OHANE I, ch. 9, §9.5 (PDF p. 578)',
  quote="the crown established estates whose land was allocated directly to loyal local functionaries ... the lack of interaction with the provincial centers and their administration points toward a deliberate separation of royal and provincial hierarchies and economies."),
 dict(param='TEMP',score=2,stance='supports',key=S,where='OHANE I, ch. 9, §9.4 (PDF p. 559)',
  quote="The organization controlled by the governor of Lagaš was very extensive; it administered large tracts of agricultural land, drew on the personnel and resources of various temples managed by temple administrators"),
 dict(param='TCTL',score=2,stance='supports',key=S,where='OHANE I, ch. 9, §9.3 (PDF p. 548)',
  quote="It is debated whether in doing so Sargon followed ancient customs or this was a new policy designed to gain control over the local clergy, but in either case he set a precedent ... A dedication to Sargon by a “temple administrator of Zabala” may imply that in addition to installing high priestesses, the king also personally appointed the heads of at least some temples."),
 dict(param='TCTL',score=3,stance='qualifies',key=S,where='OHANE I, ch. 9, §9.3 (PDF p. 551); §9.6 (PDF p. 581)',
  quote="Notably, this meant an expropriation of land originally owned by the temples ... Modern commentators ascribe great importance to the forced appropriation of land originally owned by the temples and the subsequent establishment of agricultural estates under the crown’s control because it was considered a sacrilegious seizure of divine property."),
 dict(param='TCTL',score=2,stance='supports',key=S,where='OHANE I, ch. 9, §9.4 (PDF p. 569)',
  quote="The only local official mentioned is Irinabadbi, the temple administrator. He was appointed by the king after the “Great Revolt” and called himself servant of Naram-Sin in his inscriptions."),
 dict(param='PRIV',score=1,stance='supports',key=S,where='OHANE I, ch. 9, §9.2 (PDF p. 543)',
  quote="There are some private archives that document the commercial enterprises of wealthy families ... But these private archives are few in number, and the distinction between official and private is sometimes not strictly defined."),
 dict(param='PRIV',score=2,stance='qualifies',key=S,where='OHANE I, ch. 9, §9.6 (PDF pp. 580, 582), citing Renger 1995, Steinkeller 1999a',
  quote="establishment of large agricultural estates and the spread of private tenure of land that was ultimately owned by the king ... In view of the spread of tributary patterns of economy in evidence under the Sargonic rulers, both interpretations can be understood in the context of a “privatization” of the state."),
 dict(param='BUR',score=3,stance='supports',key=S,where='OHANE I, ch. 9, §9.4 (PDF p. 556)',
  quote="There is a remarkable uniformity in the shape of the clay tablets and their layout, and the cuneiform sign forms are characterized by an elaborate ductus typically used to write Akkadian in the Classical Sargonic variant ... This typical writing style is first clearly noticeable during the reign of Naram-Sin and often thought to reflect an administrative and writing reform.",
  note='Directly about tablet shape: a published qualitative claim our data can test.'),
 dict(param='BUR',score=2,stance='qualifies',key=S,where='OHANE I, ch. 9, §9.4 (PDF p. 556)',
  quote="But as the orthographic system of Sargonic Akkadian was already used in Sargon’s royal inscriptions, and as the typical script and language co-occur along with local writing styles and dialects in various archival contexts, the absence of earlier evidence may merely be the result of a lack of preserved royal archives"),
 dict(param='BUR',score=2,stance='contests',key=S,where='OHANE I, ch. 9, §9.4 (PDF p. 565)',
  quote="The texts are dated according to local conventions with mu-iti dates ... and use local capacity measures such as the “Sumerian sila” ... as well as local sign forms, all testifying to a continuation of the local archival practices."),
 dict(param='SCHOOL',score=1,stance='supports',key=S,where='OHANE I, ch. 9, §9.2 (PDF p. 543)',
  quote="a smaller number of scribal exercises and school texts, usually lexical texts and some literary compositions, incantations, and rituals, all materials pertaining to the education of the Sargonic bureaucrats and the scribal elite."),
 dict(param='SCHOOL',score=2,stance='qualifies',key=S,where='OHANE I, ch. 9, §9.5 (PDF p. 573)',
  quote="the Sargonic Akkadian used in the texts of this archive occasionally shows indications of imperfect second-language learning, obviously as a result of its introduction as an administrative language.",
  note='Implies deliberate training of southern scribes in the royal administrative language; locus of training unknown.'),
 dict(param='LAW',score=1,stance='supports',key=S,where='OHANE I, ch. 9, §9.4 (PDF p. 562)',
  quote="followed by an unnamed majordomo ... the majordomo Dada; six generals; twelve judges; Ilum-dan, a well-known conscription officer",
  note='Judges in the royal entourage; no Sargonic law collection.'),
 dict(param='STD',score=2,stance='supports',key=S,where='OHANE I, ch. 9, §9.3 (PDF p. 549)',
  quote="In archival records from Nippur, some elements of centralization are discernible: year names of Sargon, offerings on behalf of the king, and the measurement unit “royal kor” (gur lugal), a precursor of the later “Akkadian kor” (gur A-ga-de3ki). The use of royal standards always implies an interference in the local administration"),
 dict(param='STD',score=2,stance='supports',key=S,where='OHANE I, ch. 9, §9.4 (PDF p. 566)',
  quote="are dated according to the Akkadian calendar, and use royal units of measurement such as the “Akkadian sila” and “Akkadian kor” ... instead of local ones, so they were clearly composed within the royal sphere of authority."),
 dict(param='STD',score=1,stance='contests',key=S,where='OHANE I, ch. 9, §9.3 (PDF p. 549)',
  quote="The archive of the governor of Umma still used the local mu-iti dating system ... Meskigala, the governor of Adab, even used his own date formulae, which indicates a notable degree of independence, as the promulgation of year names was normally considered a royal prerogative."),
]
GAPS={'BUR':'Sargonic administrative reform and tablet format: Foster 1982 (Umma in the Sargonic Period); Westenholz 1999 (OBO 160/3); Sommerfeld 2003; Foster 2016 (The Age of Agade); Maiocchi 2015 (palaeography)',
      'SCHOOL':'Sargonic school texts: Westenholz 1974-77; Foster 1982h',
      'FISC':'Steinkeller 1993 (Early political development); Foster 2016 ch. 3 on tribute and taxation'}
